# Workspace

- Repositories live in `repos/<name>/`. If the user asks for a repository that isn't there, clone it into `repos/` with `git clone`. (`add_repo` is for cloud sessions and does nothing here.)
- Don't edit files in `repos/<name>/` directly. For each task, create a worktree and work only there:
  `git -C repos/<name> worktree add ../../worktrees/<name>-<branch> -b <branch>`
- Name the branch after the task. Reuse an existing worktree if the user asks to continue earlier work.
- When the user says a task is finished, remove its worktree with `git -C repos/<name> worktree remove <path>`.
- Push your work to a branch and open a pull request with `gh pr create`. Don't push to the default branch.

# Installing tools

- If a CLI tool is missing, try installing it to user space yourself before asking. Don't ask first, and don't go looking for brew, podman, docker, apt or sudo; none are available or intended here.
- Preferred order: `mise use -g <backend>:<tool>` (for example `github:BurntSushi/ripgrep`, `aqua:jqlang/jq`, `ubi:`), then `uv tool install` for Python tools, then `npm install -g` with a user prefix for Node tools.
- Installs must stay inside the sandbox's write paths (`~/.local/share/mise`, `~/.config/mise`, `~/.cache`, `~/.npm`) and the allowlisted hosts (GitHub, npm, PyPI, nodejs.org, mise hosts).
- If the install fails on a sandbox write restriction or a blocked host, stop and tell the user which path or host it hit. Don't work around it, and don't try containers or system-wide installs.

# Sharing artifacts with the user

- Share a report, review page or any document for the user to look at as a **Claude Doc**, not a file path: a path only opens on web or desktop, and a doc also opens in the Claude app on mobile. Use the `mcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__*` tools.
- Make the doc first: the turn's first doc call is `batch` with `container: {kind: "project", create: {name, doc: {blocks, markdown}}}` and `batch: []`. The markdown is the title, the byline `<?claude block asof?> · <?claude block me?>` (an as-of date chip and a mention of the user) and one `pending` block per section. Open it for the user with the `Artifact` tool's `open` action and the link the birth reply gives (`https://claude.ai/artifact/...`), in your next message, and give the link once. Then fill one section per `update` (`replace` the pending block with `## heading` and the body, `as: "markdown"`), reading `guide(items=["topic.index"])` once first.
- Content that works: prose, pipe tables, bullet and task lists, and links. Link drawings and pages to GitHub (`https://github.com/jeottesen/<repo>/blob/main/...`) so they open from a phone. **Images work** (confirmed 2026-11-11, 12 images in one doc). A doc only takes images uploaded to it, and an image from a web address is refused (`unsupported_construct`). Do it last, after every section has its text and no `pending` block is left: (1) upload the local files with the `Artifact` tool: `action: "publish"`, `asset: true`, `url` = the doc's link, `file_paths` = up to 25 absolute paths; each result gives an asset id (32 hex); (2) one `batch` on the doc with a member per image `{"$lid": "b1", "verb": "create", "object": "blob", "engine": "blob", "payload": {"asset": "<asset id>"}}`; the reply's `lids` map each `$lid` to the blob id; (3) one `update` of the prose body with `insert` ops, `side: "after"` the caption paragraph, `source: {from: {kind: "inline", content: "![alt](blob/<blob id>)"}, as: "markdown"}`. Read `guide(items=["topic.uploads"])` first.
- After filling it, leave one comment anchored on a passage you wrote that asks the user to confirm an assumption or choose between two options: load the tool with `ToolSearch` (`select:mcp__claude_ai_Claude_Docs__create`), then `create` an `utterance` with the anchor `{kind: "find", text: "...", nth: 1}` (a phrase that occurs twice needs `nth`). Leave no other unprompted comments.
- Keep editing the same doc for follow-ups; don't make a new one. Don't edit a doc the user says they are still reading until they are done, unless they ask.
- `SendUserFile` and other attachments do not work in this session (no thread to receive them). A local HTML file under `/home/claude/workspace/` is only a desktop fallback; say so if you use one.